            <div type="translation" resp="#martaf">
                <p>Tomb of Artem… and of his … fare you well.</p>
            </div>
            <div type="commentary" resp="#martaf">
                <p>This inscription is highly fragmentary: most editors have interpreted it as a funerary inscription. Torremuzza’s reading (1784 cl. XVIII no. 33) of l. 1 ΘΗ, together with the presence of two genitives in ll. 3–4, leaves no room for alternative interpretations: <foreign xml:lang="grc">θήκη</foreign> is also used in other funerary inscriptions in the sense of “tomb” (e.g. IG XIV 633, 783). Ferrua (1989, 113–114 no. 428) identifies this term as a Christian word and integrates a christogram at the end of the inscription. Kaibel’s reading (ap. IG XIV 452) of l. 1 <foreign xml:lang="grc">Θε[οῖς πᾶσι</foreign>, besides being erroneous, is at the root of an incorrect interpretation of the document’s typology, which the scholar considered to be a dedicatory epigraph; this interpretation, however, conflicts with the genitives at ll. 3–4. It is therefore preferable to understand the text as an epitaph, for a deceased individual whose name is only partially preserved and which scholars have variously restored as <foreign xml:lang="grc">Ἀρτέμων</foreign> (Franz ap. CIG III 5572; Manni Piraino ap. IGMusPalermo 6) or <foreign xml:lang="grc">Ἀρτεμίσιος</foreign> (Sandberg 1954, 41), and for one of his relatives (the son according to Franz ap. CIG III 5572; the brother according to Sandberg 1954, 41; or the grandson according to Manni Piraino ap. IGMusPalermo 6). The last line has received several readings: Kaibel (ap. IG XIV 452), assuming it to be a dedication, read <foreign xml:lang="grc">εὐπλοίας ἔνεκα</foreign>, the expression used to thank the gods for a safe voyage, whereas Alföldi (1976, 511), although agreeing on the funerary destination of the text, was perhaps influenced by Kaibel’s interpretation and read a proper name (<foreign xml:lang="grc">Εὔπλους</foreign> or <foreign xml:lang="grc">Εὐπλία</foreign>), understood as the dedicant. For Ferrua (1989, 113–114 no. 428), this was the proper name of the deceased’s son, <foreign xml:lang="grc">Εὔπλου</foreign>. Sandberg (1954, 41–42), supported by Manni Piraino (ap. IGMusPalermo 6), read the imperative <foreign xml:lang="grc">εὔπλοει</foreign> or <foreign xml:lang="grc">εὐπλοεῖτε</foreign> as a wish addressed to the passer-by, similar to <foreign xml:lang="grc">εὐτύχει</foreign> or <foreign xml:lang="grc">εὐτυχεῖτε</foreign>: the plural form is also found in IG XIV 933 l. 11 (Portus, 1st–2nd century AD), while in the singular the contracted form <foreign xml:lang="grc">εὐπλόι</foreign> is more common (IG XIV 2409, 8; SEG 42.942 l. 2; IG XIV 2472 l. 9 = CIL XII 758).</p>
            </div>
